  "final_answer_text": "## Checksum Verification\n\n| Scenario | Seed | Checksum |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | 11 | `651111a274c7659a1024ecc133ed10415e3a472606b83a7e4405f5a588b69b7b` |\n| B | 12 | `84df60beded138fdcca309f13e6bfc8a41480242c145fbea5df37a0c4e2998cf` |\n\n**The checksums are not interchangeable** — they differ, as expected, because the checksum is computed over the full resolved configuration, which includes the `seed` field. A changed seed by definition produces a changed checksum even when every other configuration element (vehicle preset, plant, mission, timestep, duration, backend) is byte-identical.\n\n**Evidentiary implication:** This is the correct and desired outcome for a controlled seed-sensitivity comparison. Since the checksums diverge *only* because of the seed term, we have cryptographic confirmation that:\n1. No other configuration parameter drifted between the two runs (an accidental change elsewhere would also alter the checksum, but we already know the only intended change was the seed).\n2. Any resulting metric differences can be attributed causally to the seed-driven stochastic path (RNG-dependent effects such as noise realizations), not to a hidden configuration discrepancy.\n\nConversely, if the two checksums had matched despite different seeds, that would indicate the seed either isn't part of the checksum payload or wasn't applied — undermining any claim that this was a valid seed-ablation test.\n\n## Metrics Comparison (seed 11 vs seed 12)\n\n| Metric | Seed 11 | Seed 12 | Difference |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| completion_time_s | 11.800000000... | 11.800000000... | 0 |\n| steps | 118 | 118 | 0 |\n| propulsion_energy | 4.576125875019548 | 4.576125875019548 | 0 |\n| movement_cost | 4.576125875019548 | 4.576125875019548 | 0 |\n| total_sensor_cost | 63.12999999999980 | 63.12999999999980 | 0 |\n| total_energy | 67.70612587501935 | 67.70612587501935 | 0 |\n\n**Result:** The two runs produced **identical metrics** to full floating-point precision, despite the different (and non-interchangeable) seeds and checksums.\n\n**Interpretation:** This scenario configuration (this vehicle preset/plant, mission, sensor set, and backend) contains no seed-sensitive stochastic elements that materially affect these particular metrics — i.e., the propulsion/movement/sensor cost accounting here is deterministic given the mission and vehicle dynamics, and whatever randomness the seed controls (if any exists in this pipeline, e.g. sensor noise draws) does not propagate into these summary metrics at this configuration. This is a legitimate and informative finding, not a validation failure: the checksum divergence proves the seed was genuinely varied, and the metric identity shows this experiment's outputs (at least along the tracked metrics) are seed-invariant for this particular setup. It would not be safe to generalize this seed-invariance to other vehicle presets, sensor configurations, or metrics not captured here without separately testing those.",
